CAT = "sgm9aCase"; SEG = "CAS"
Q = [
('Mei, 68, needs steady monthly income and access to her savings. A 6-year structured ILP is:',
 'Unsuitable', ['Ideal for steady income', 'Suitable if capped', 'Suitable with a top-up'],
 'A long, single premium structure with payoffs at maturity does not meet a need for regular income and access, so the recommendation would fail a suitability test.'),
('Raj accepts loss of capital for higher returns and has never traded derivatives. Before selling him a structured ILP, the adviser must assess his:',
 'Investment knowledge', ['Medical history', "Employer's approval", 'Property holdings'],
 'Structured ILPs are Specified Investment Products, so under FAA-N16 the adviser must check whether the client has the relevant knowledge or experience.'),
('A note pays 80% of an index rise, capped at 24%. The index rises 25%. What is paid?',
 '20%', ['24%', '25%', '30%'],
 '80% x 25% = 20%, which is below the 24% cap, so 20% is paid. The cap matters only when 80% of the rise exceeds 24%.'),
('A note pays 75% of an index rise, capped at 18%. The index rises 30%. What is paid?',
 '18%', ['22.5%', '30%', '12%'],
 '75% x 30% = 22.5%, which is above the cap, so the return is limited to 18%.'),
("A reverse convertible on a share starting at S$5.00 has a strike of S$4.00. The share ends at S$3.20. How much of the share's value per S$4.00 of capital is received?",
 'S$3.20', ['S$4.00', 'S$5.00', 'S$0.80'],
 'Below the strike the investor receives shares, one per S$4.00 of capital, now worth S$3.20 each. The loss of S$0.80 a share is offset only by the coupon.'),
('An autocallable pays 8% a year and is called once the index is at or above its start level on an annual date. In year 1 the index is up 3%. The note:',
 'Is called with 8% paid', ['Pays 3% and runs on', 'Pays 11% and runs on', 'Is called with 3% paid'],
 'The trigger is met, so the note ends after year 1 and pays the capital plus the 8% coupon. Upside beyond the coupon is not shared.'),
("An autocallable note is called early in a strong market. The investor's main problem is most likely:",
 'Reinvestment', ['A credit event loss', 'Paying a margin call', 'Losing the death benefit'],
 'Early calls tend to happen in good markets, returning money just when similar coupons may be lower, which is reinvestment risk.'),
('Mr Tan bought a capital-protected structured ILP for S$50,000 three years ago. Markets fell and he wants to surrender. The adviser should warn that:',
 'He may get less than S$50,000', ['He gets S$50,000 at once', 'MAS will make up losses', 'The death benefit is paid out'],
 'Protection applies at maturity only. A surrender now is at the current unit value, after any charges, which may be below the premium.'),
('A client in Singapore buys a note paying in AUD. The AUD falls 10% against SGD over the term while the note pays in full. Her SGD result:',
 'Falls about 10%', ['Rises by about 10%', 'Is unaffected', 'Is protected by SDIC'],
 'The AUD amounts are converted at a weaker rate, so the SGD value is about 10% lower than if the exchange rate had held.'),
('A structured deposit pays the full principal at maturity plus 60% of any index rise. The index falls 20%. The depositor receives:',
 'The principal only', ['80% of principal', '60% of principal', 'Principal plus 12%'],
 "There is no rise to participate in, so only the principal is repaid at maturity, subject to the bank's credit risk."),
("A client wants a structured product because a friend earned 15% on one. The adviser's best first step is to:",
 'Explain the payoff', ['Place the same product', 'Promise a similar return', 'Recommend the largest one'],
 "Past returns of another product do not show the client's risk. The adviser must explain the formula, risks and worst case before any recommendation."),
("A sub-fund's note issuer is downgraded from AA to BBB. A policyholder asks what this means. The best answer is that:",
 'Default risk has risen', ['The payoff is now guaranteed', 'Coupons will rise at once', 'Nothing has changed'],
 'A lower rating signals a higher chance of default, and the payoff depends on the issuer paying. The unit value may also fall.'),
('Two capital-protected products run 3 and 7 years. All else equal, the 7-year one usually has:',
 'More participation', ['Lower credit risk', 'Better liquidity', 'A lower floor'],
 'A longer term makes the zero-coupon bond cheaper, leaving more to spend on options, so participation can be higher. The longer lock-up adds liquidity and credit risk.'),
('Sara holds a barrier note: capital back at maturity unless the index ever falls below 70% of its start. It drops to 65%, then recovers to 95%. She gets:',
 '95% of capital', ['100% of capital', '65% of capital', '70% of capital'],
 "The barrier was breached, so the protection fell away and she takes the index's final performance: 95% of capital."),
('A note repays capital unless its index ever falls below 60% of the start. The index dips to 64% and ends at 90%. The investor gets:',
 'All its capital', ['90% of capital', '64% of capital', 'Capital plus 10%'],
 'The 60% barrier was never touched, so the protection stays and the full capital is repaid even though the index ended lower.'),
('A credit-linked note references Company X. Company X defaults with a 40% recovery. Ignoring coupons, an investor of S$10,000 gets back about:',
 'S$4,000', ['S$6,000', 'S$10,000', 'S$0'],
 'On a credit event the investor bears the loss on the reference entity: repayment follows the 40% recovery, so about S$4,000 of S$10,000.'),
('A client says "I want no chance of losing money and full access any time". Which of these products fits?',
 'None of these products', ['A barrier note', 'A capped structured ILP', 'A reverse convertible'],
 'Every structured product carries issuer risk and lock-in. A client needing certainty and liquidity is better served by deposits or similar products.'),
('A client compares a structured ILP with a 2% deposit. Over 5 years the ILP returns capital only. Her opportunity cost is about:',
 'Forgone interest', ['Nothing at all', 'The full premium', "The ILP's death benefit"],
 'Getting only capital back means missing what the deposit would have earned, roughly 2% a year compounded over five years.'),
("A fund manager uses index futures to cut a fund's equity exposure during a crisis. This use of derivatives is:",
 'Hedging', ['Speculation', 'Market manipulation', 'Arbitrage'],
 'Selling index futures against a stock portfolio offsets part of its market risk. The aim is to reduce risk, not to profit from the futures.'),
('A sub-fund buys 10 index futures, each worth S$100,000, under the Commitment Approach. Its derivative exposure is:',
 'S$1,000,000', ['S$100,000', 'S$10,000', 'The margin paid'],
 'CIS Code Table 1 values index futures at number of contracts x value of 1 point x index level, i.e. the full contract value: 10 x S$100,000 = S$1,000,000.'),
('A sub-fund with NAV S$20 million already has derivative exposure of S$18 million. Under the CIS Code, how much more exposure may it add?',
 'S$2 million', ['S$18 million', 'S$20 million', 'S$0'],
 'Global exposure may not exceed 100% of NAV, S$20 million, so only S$2 million more is allowed.'),
('A sub-fund of NAV S$30 million has OTC exposure to an unrated bank with no guarantee. The most it may have with that bank is:',
 'S$1.5 million', ['S$3 million', 'S$30 million', 'S$0.3 million'],
 'A counterparty that is not an eligible financial institution is limited to 5% of NAV: 5% x S$30 million = S$1.5 million.'),
('A CPPI fund worth 100 has a floor value of 90 and a multiplier of 5. The risky asset then falls 10% before rebalancing. The fund is worth about:',
 '95', ['90', '100', '50'],
 'Exposure = 5 x 10 = 50 in the risky asset. A 10% fall costs 5, so the fund drops to 95, still above the floor of 90.'),
('A CPPI fund worth 100 holds 50 in a risky asset, with a floor value of 90. The risky asset falls 25% overnight. The fund ends at:',
 '87.5', ['95', '90', '75'],
 'A 25% fall on 50 loses 12.5, leaving 87.5, below the floor of 90. This is gap risk: the drop came before the fund could rebalance.'),
('A client buys a structured ILP and later learns the agent earned a high commission on it. The disclosure issue concerns:',
 'Conflicts of interest', ['The free-look period', "The guarantor's rating", 'The index method'],
 'Remuneration that could sway a recommendation is a conflict of interest that must be disclosed so the client can judge the advice.'),
('An adviser shows only the best case return of a structured deposit in a brochure. Under FAA-G09 this is:',
 'Not fair disclosure', ['Acceptable if accurate', 'Required by MAS', 'A worst-case illustration'],
 'FAA-G09 expects illustrations of both best and worst cases and disclosure of all risk factors that could reduce benefits.'),
("A sub-fund's guarantor goes into liquidation (not a voluntary reconstruction). Under the guarantee terms, the guarantee:",
 'May be terminated', ['Doubles in value', 'Passes to MAS', 'Becomes a deposit'],
 'The CIS Code Appendix 4 and Notice 307 Annex Ac list liquidation of the guarantor as an event on which the guarantee may end.'),
('A client wants equity upside, can lock money away 5 years and accepts issuer risk, but cannot accept losing capital in a crash. A fitting product is:',
 'Protected note', ['A reverse convertible', 'A long CFD position', 'An uncovered call'],
 'Protection at maturity plus participation in rises matches these needs. Reverse convertibles and CFDs can lose capital in a crash, and writing calls adds unlimited risk.'),
]
